all right let's get into audience  questions for the week one day early  because of my upcoming trip remember  we're going to have some great guest  hosts upcoming including farron cousins  from ring of fire and fair and balance  the eponymously named fair and balanced  um also jesse dallimore it's going to be  great it's going to be absolutely  fantastic but let's get right into your  questions david do you think joe biden  is really going to try to pass a  national vaccine mandate the statements  so far from the white house have been  confusing yeah so  the white house i think is being careful  there's been a couple of times now where  white house staffers have been asked  about  is by investigating is biden planning  for is he getting  advice about a possible vaccine mandate  and the answer has been a pretty  carefully worded that is not something  he is looking to do or has any plans to  do at this time  and of course  when i hear that i don't hear  he's eventually going to do it i hear  he's not ruling it out unconditionally  because circumstances may change and  it's conceivable that it makes sense to  leave it on the table but i actually  don't think the biden administration is  going to even attempt to pass a federal  vaccine mandate  however we've already talked about the  potential legality of state-level  vaccine mandates and municipality level  vaccine mandates and there does seem to  be precedent for it you look at  cambridge massachusetts in the very  early 20th century i don't remember if  it was 1905 1903 there was a point where  the the city of cambridge said you must  be vaccinated against smallpox the  vaccine was quote experimental at the  time much the same way that now the  vaccines have not yet received fda  approval although they are expected too  soon and so my guess would be  that if joe biden wanted to try to  force people to be vaccinated and again  that doesn't mean someone shows up and  holds you down  my guess is that it would be some kind  of requirement with i don't know maybe a  fine attached to it or something even  saying it doesn't really make any sense  because i don't think it's going to  happen i think the way biden would do it  is he would push  for governors or municipalities  to mandate it which seems  much more  um  based in precedent at least from a legal  standpoint now i'll go even further  i don't know that we're really going to  see that  because i i think accurately it's seen  as likely political suicide for whoever  tries to do it i think even in blue  states  with  democratic governors  a vaccine mandate could be a recipe for  you lose your next reelection  because i think it would not be well  received by enough people in basically  every state  that it could be a really politically  disastrous thing to do  and so more plausible  and maybe equally effective is just look  at the polling where people say what  would be most likely to encourage them  to get vaccinated so as an example  last week i covered this live event  where joe biden said hey governors  please use the money we sent you to give  a hundred dollars to everybody who goes  and gets a first shot okay  a poll was done on that over the weekend  and only 14 of americans who are not  interested in the vaccine said 100 would  change their mind 14 said i don't want  the vaccine but for a hundred bucks i  would go  contrast that  with 41  of those who don't currently want a  vaccine who said if it was required to  fly  i would go and get vaccinated it doesn't  take  that much thinking to realize  joe biden and the federal government  through the faa  uh have a lot of say over what happens  on airplanes  and would there be  a more effective approach than mandating  vaccines or giving people 100 bucks  then working through the faa  either to directly or to encourage  airlines to say you must be vaccinated  in order to fly with us or at minimum  if you're not vaccinated you have to do  a negative test  uh you could even i think even that  would significantly increase vaccination  numbers right now when you fly  domestically there might be some  examples like i know at one point hawaii  was doing something with testing  right now though to fly domestically you  don't even need to be tested so you  could even stop short of a vaccine  requirement for for aviation domestic  aviation you could just say you now have  two options when you fly you either  bring proof of vaccination  uh which i know some people will say  well people will start faking those  that's got to be punished really  seriously really really seriously the  way canada punished a couple of  americans for faking vaccine information  um you either give us proof of  vaccination or you've got to get a test  right here  or within 48 72 however you figure it  out  that would make a really big dent in  those who don't want to get vaccinated  go by what people are telling you is the  point that i'm trying to make i don't  expect joe biden to actually try a  federal vaccine mandate i think if he  wants to go in that direction it's  likely he would say governors please do  this in your states of course if he did  that  the governors that are willing to do it  are probably the ones in the states that  are already more vaccinated and less  urgently would need to do it that's  always that's sort of a moral hazard  status quo type of thing but that's the  way i see it let me know if you see the  likelihood of a biden vaccine mandate  differently  if you ever feel like you just don't  have enough time to read all the books  you want to read you have to check out  one of my favorite apps called blinkist  blinkist takes thousands of popular  nonfiction books and distills each one  down into an e-book or audiobook that  you can get through in just 15 minutes  where you're getting all the key  takeaways from the book just imagine how  you'll be able to expand your horizons  and knowledge by being able to soak up  all of the important insights from 10  different books  in an afternoon obviously it's perfect  for exposing yourself to a new book you  otherwise wouldn't have time for or you  can use it to revisit a book you've  already read or use it to preview a book  before you buy the full version and read  it i recently read a brief history of  time of course by the great stephen  hawking this is a book that i have been  aware of for so long and other things  got in the way and it was fantastic to  check it out on blinkist blinkist has  books on politics philosophy science  they have 27 different non-fiction  categories and a subscription is only  about eight bucks a month and you get  the entire library but you can try it  totally free and get twenty five percent  off a subscription when you go to  blinkist.com  pacman i've put the link right  underneath this video

